<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Martyrdom of Saint Shushanik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik is a hagiography written in the late fifth century by the priest Iakob Tsurtaveli, the oldest surviving work of Georgian literature. It tells of Shushanik, an Armenian princess married to Varsken, the pitiakhsh of southern Kartli, who was imprisoned and tortured to death for refusing to follow her husband's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martyrdom-of-the-holy-queen-shushanik.jpg" alt="Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikhail Sabinin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sabinin._St._Shushanik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work tells the martyrdom of Saint Shushanik, consort of Varsken, the bidaxshe or high prince who ruled Gogarene and had renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Shushanik was the daughter of Vardan Mamikonyan, the sparapet or military leader of the Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. She refused to follow her husband and died a martyr after years of imprisonment and torture. Its author, Iakob Tsurtaveli, was a contemporary of the events he describes and a participant in them, and the work is sometimes called a hagiographic novel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The text is held to have been written between 476 and 483 and survives in eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The oldest, the Parkhali Mravaltavi, is presumably a 10th-century codex copied at Parkhali Monastery; it holds only the first 12 chapters, because its 16th quire of eight folios was lost at an unknown date, taking the final third of the narrative with it. A short recension survives in the 10th-century manuscript Ivir. georg. 57 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, and a concise liturgical version entered the Georgian Synaxarion.</p><p>Mikhail Sabinin first printed the long text in 1882. Sargis Gorgadze published a critical edition from four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in 1917 and Ilia Abuladze one from eight in 1938, followed in 1963 by an edition using all eleven. Aleksandre Khakhanashvili edited the Athos version in 1910 with so many transcription errors that Abuladze republished it in 1938 from photographs of the codex. A new scholarly edition with commentary and facsimiles of the main manuscripts appeared in 2025.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The work has been translated into Russian, French, English, German, Spanish, Hungarian and Icelandic, and in 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> marked the 1,500th anniversary of the Martyrdom. A passage of The Conversion of Kartli, the narrative of the Cross of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nino">Saint Nino</a>, also recounts Shushanik's death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Asomtavruli, was in use by the fifth century, as shown by inscriptions at Bir el Qutt near Bethlehem and at the Bolnisi Sioni church, dated 493–494, and the <b>Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik</b>, written in the late fifth century, is the oldest surviving work of Georgian literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first direct records are 5th-century inscriptions and palimpsests, manuscripts whose first text was scraped off and written over, and its oldest surviving literary work is the 5th-century <b>Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik</b> by Iakob Tsurtaveli.</blockquote><a href="/language/georgian">Georgian · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
